  "notes": "Seen from the left the fin's bands fall toward the leading edge; the right side matches (mirrored). The belly split is level from chin to tail; the far underside isn't seen in these photos beyond the blue. Small red stickers on the engines and the registration aren't drawn. Mid-2020s A320neos and A350s match the older A320s. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on G-EUYW (fitted to the A320 shape) and G-TTNR: the title sits higher and smaller than drawn (its cap top just under the windows, about 0.6 m tall) and starts at x 0.163; the coat of arms sits just aft of its end, x 0.36; the fin's ribbons drawn as curves. On the A380 (G-XLED, G-XLEC, G-XLEF; sources 5-7) the title, Speedmarque and coat of arms sit between the decks, the belly's blue ends where the fuselage sweeps up and the fin's red runs aft to the tail cone; drawn on the A380's shape they come from `types.A388`, measured there. The A380 isn't BA's commonest type, so drawnOn stays.",
  "history": "Replaces Skyarium v1's approximation. Chatham Dockyard (Union Flag) first flew in 1997 on Concorde and replaced the World Images ethnic tails fleet-wide by 2001; the earlier Landor scheme survives only as a retro (landor-retro).",
